{"title":"2005 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 20","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>While driving at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/3/c83e2ba079edb9225644712d7f35022491005f2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80 km/h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" />, Sally’s car passes a hydro pole every four seconds. Which of the following is closest to the distance between two neighbouring hydro poles?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/2/de2e65210108499a736b20f0d8e79e8ce6e51384.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 50 m \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 60 m \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 70 m \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 80 m \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 90 m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"455\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since Sally’s car travels <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/3/c83e2ba079edb9225644712d7f35022491005f2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80 km/h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" />, it travels <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/2/4021076c9ebf92fd1587a453b8134758e77820e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80,000 m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"69\" height=\"16\" /> in one hour.\nSince there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/302e99c1c7cd91a6ae371139e5142ccef5977dca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> minutes in an hour, the car travels <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/c/44c2f219130904997ac02e5b24ec5f41910b7f3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{60}\\times 80,000 m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"113\" height=\"37\" /> in one minute.\nSince there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/302e99c1c7cd91a6ae371139e5142ccef5977dca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> seconds in a minute, the car travels <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b82a9693f000b5d10e5bf29b99a935d0949a73f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{60}\\times \\frac{1}{60}\\times 80,000 m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"157\" height=\"37\" /> in one second.\nTherefore, in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> seconds, the car travels <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/a/d0a0e4e9990da518d4fd3adc03549bcca9986136.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\times \\frac{1}{60}\\times \\frac{1}{60}\\times 80,000 = 88.89 m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"254\" height=\"37\" />.\nOf the possible choices, this is closest to 90 m.  Therefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</spa